{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2171 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "442755ac43b7682183b4ae0fcea064d8852b65d12ebff6533b4032eae3e408fe" :edb/db-before-t 2170 :edb/db-after-t 2171 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535483 50 #inst "2026-10-09T01:00:17.554Z" 13194139535483 true] [17592186133805 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351516 13194139535483 true] [17592186133806 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351523 13194139535483 true] [17592186133806 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351530 13194139535483 true] [17592186133807 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351537 13194139535483 true] [17592186133807 1020 #edb/ref 17592186351544 13194139535483 true] [17592186351516 1017 #edb/ref 17592186351517 13194139535483 true] [17592186351516 1089 "q-109130" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351516 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535483 true] [17592186351516 1100 "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,2),(1,0)$ and $(2,0),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})\\,dx+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nCalculate the function $f(x,y).$" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351516 1113 #uuid "6a0fcc5b-0558-5150-a8c6-d2887d157c75" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351516 1145 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066044/assets/8e3efc3f9c58fb0f9a84144e97a300b6ab2a4011d47ee0d2bae1edc8a3f721cd.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})\\,dx+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351517 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351518 13194139535483 true] [17592186351517 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351519 13194139535483 true] [17592186351517 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351520 13194139535483 true] [17592186351517 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351521 13194139535483 true] [17592186351517 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351522 13194139535483 true] [17592186351517 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535483 true] [17592186351517 1041 "selection" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351517 1104 #edb/ref 17592186351519 13194139535483 true] [17592186351517 1110 #uuid "99195091-e352-5269-9cd2-10d1dde94606" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351518 1034 "-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351518 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535483 true] [17592186351518 1111 #uuid "9c386041-9e1c-58c3-8b3e-f5b308070b54" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351519 1034 "\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351519 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535483 true] [17592186351519 1111 #uuid "2f714b0e-a5cc-54fb-b099-b1ad69ac4e04" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351520 1034 "\\frac{x+y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351520 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535483 true] [17592186351520 1111 #uuid "4fbc58e1-7638-53ef-96ef-0fafc474c5fc" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351521 1034 "-\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351521 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535483 true] [17592186351521 1111 #uuid "73a3bf70-1ff5-56a2-be1b-298dfbcfc8a7" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351522 1034 "\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351522 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535483 true] [17592186351522 1111 #uuid "8d74c7c2-955b-58c1-9754-9f01792a9be6" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351523 1017 #edb/ref 17592186351524 13194139535483 true] [17592186351523 1089 "q-109151" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351523 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535483 true] [17592186351523 1100 "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$ where $F(x,y)=⟨{e}^{x}-{y}^{2},{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}⟩$ and $C$ is the boundary of the square with vertices at $(-1,-1),$ $(1,-1),$ $(1,1)$ and $(-1,1).$" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351523 1113 #uuid "f4dd77af-0cc1-5c9d-aba4-c3fd1a97e97d" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351523 1145 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066044/assets/574172dda3031b9bb44dbcfb46265453ae543a75bccf8db24e93625e7154bba9.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}({e}^{x}-{y}^{2})\\,dx+({e}^{y}+{x}^{2})\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={e}^{x}-{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={e}^{y}+{x}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({e}^{x}-{y}^{2})\\,dx+({e}^{y}+{x}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(2x+2y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[2xy+{y}^{2}]}_{-1}^{1}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(2x(1)+(1)^{2}-2x(-1)-(-1)^{2})\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}4x\\,dx \\\\  & =2{[{x}^{2}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =2[(1)^{2}-(-1)^{2}] \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351524 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351525 13194139535483 true] [17592186351524 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351526 13194139535483 true] [17592186351524 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351527 13194139535483 true] [17592186351524 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351528 13194139535483 true] [17592186351524 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351529 13194139535483 true] [17592186351524 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535483 true] [17592186351524 1041 "selection" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351524 1104 #edb/ref 17592186351529 13194139535483 true] [17592186351524 1110 #uuid "ee53ccd9-f545-57f5-b44a-a9c3587fd91e" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351525 1034 "-\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351525 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535483 true] [17592186351525 1111 #uuid "d3aae30a-5e41-5474-8a31-6d148951bc69" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351526 1034 "\\frac{2}{3}" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351526 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535483 true] [17592186351526 1111 #uuid "addb2e9b-c846-554e-9300-422f5f8be413" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351527 1034 "-3" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351527 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535483 true] [17592186351527 1111 #uuid "62bae293-97dd-5fcf-a6b8-aded44d3a2bd" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351528 1034 "4" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351528 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535483 true] [17592186351528 1111 #uuid "5d0ac646-c124-5e6f-a171-a0ed5363dc84" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351529 1034 "0" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351529 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535483 true] [17592186351529 1111 #uuid "78f09a34-4196-581e-977b-e5b599f3b562" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351530 1017 #edb/ref 17592186351531 13194139535483 true] [17592186351530 1089 "q-109166" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351530 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535483 true] [17592186351530 1100 "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$ where $F(x,y)=⟨2x+{y}^{2},{x}^{2}+2y⟩$ and $C$ is the boundary of the region enclosed by the curve $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}$ and the lines $y=0$ and $x=3.$" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351530 1113 #uuid "d92cf6d2-0783-508f-af3a-287ef5da8ee9" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351530 1145 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066044/assets/fafd281fc63fb276c9759fd84f722096d494aa3e3fe9441f43aa6ecd1f9dfda9.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(2x+{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+2y)\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2x+{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}+2y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(2x+{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+2y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x-2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}/3}(2x-2y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{[2xy-{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{{x}^{2}/3}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{9})dx \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{6}-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{45}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3)^{4}}{6}-\\frac{(3)^{5}}{45} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{2}-\\frac{27}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351531 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351532 13194139535483 true] [17592186351531 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351533 13194139535483 true] [17592186351531 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351534 13194139535483 true] [17592186351531 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351535 13194139535483 true] [17592186351531 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351536 13194139535483 true] [17592186351531 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535483 true] [17592186351531 1041 "selection" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351531 1104 #edb/ref 17592186351533 13194139535483 true] [17592186351531 1110 #uuid "17fe58b7-a2f7-5e1e-a35c-276d06a5ea08" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351532 1034 "\\frac{27}{2}" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351532 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535483 true] [17592186351532 1111 #uuid "ec24f900-45a6-57e4-a4b1-a68b3b11e556" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351533 1034 "\\frac{81}{10}" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351533 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535483 true] [17592186351533 1111 #uuid "2d1a0da9-b07b-5a63-9844-68f1b729f0d7" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351534 1034 "\\frac{27}{5}" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351534 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535483 true] [17592186351534 1111 #uuid "53b50e0b-1685-5511-adcf-6cb080ed03b3" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351535 1034 "-\\frac{64}{5}" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351535 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535483 true] [17592186351535 1111 #uuid "acede4b9-07ea-5c6b-aaf1-8f003b260d50" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351536 1034 "-\\frac{27}{15}" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351536 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535483 true] [17592186351536 1111 #uuid "c776220c-b6ec-5360-88c8-9547ac477cd6" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351537 1017 #edb/ref 17592186351538 13194139535483 true] [17592186351537 1089 "q-109249" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351537 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535483 true] [17592186351537 1100 "Consider the closed curve $C,$ which is the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $x=y$ and $x=4y-{y}^{2}.$ Express ${∮}_{C}({e}^{y}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,dx+(2x{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,dy$ as a repeated integral." 13194139535483 true] [17592186351537 1113 #uuid "6909bb91-13a3-5626-9d74-7589d0e445fd" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351537 1145 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066044/assets/239867aec8cff7840667d2f4eafa9bc97d4fc5af09b684d863fef06fd8fd28ad.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={e}^{y}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)=2x{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={e}^{y}+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2{e}^{y}+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):y\\le x\\le 4y-{y}^{2},\\,0\\le y\\le 3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({e}^{y}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,dx+(2x{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2{e}^{y}+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-{e}^{y}-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{y}^{4y-{y}^{2}}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351538 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351539 13194139535483 true] [17592186351538 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351540 13194139535483 true] [17592186351538 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351541 13194139535483 true] [17592186351538 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351542 13194139535483 true] [17592186351538 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351543 13194139535483 true] [17592186351538 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535483 true] [17592186351538 1041 "selection" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351538 1104 #edb/ref 17592186351540 13194139535483 true] [17592186351538 1110 #uuid "c819e7d3-8e3d-5242-a37b-f5103a40944f" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351539 1034 "{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{y}^{4y-{y}^{2}}({e}^{y}-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351539 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535483 true] [17592186351539 1111 #uuid "6fb682f6-f8de-52e4-9d26-c76db4d788f5" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351540 1034 "{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{y}^{4y-{y}^{2}}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351540 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535483 true] [17592186351540 1111 #uuid "47bcc0ab-20d8-5546-a834-0f22aa069a4b" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351541 1034 "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{4y-{y}^{2}}x{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351541 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535483 true] [17592186351541 1111 #uuid "3f684af2-7293-5eb0-8f7c-7ae32d212c2f" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351542 1034 "{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{y}^{4y-{y}^{2}}(2x{e}^{y}-1)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351542 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535483 true] [17592186351542 1111 #uuid "5127880c-4426-5aaf-a60b-397ebad58a22" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351543 1034 "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{3y-{y}^{2}}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351543 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535483 true] [17592186351543 1111 #uuid "3ff8cdb0-23f3-50d0-9d7e-d0fee963e7cc" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351544 1017 #edb/ref 17592186351545 13194139535483 true] [17592186351544 1089 "q-41702" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351544 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535483 true] [17592186351544 1100 "Consider the closed curve $C,$ which is the boundary of the region enclosed by $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and the $x$-axis, for $\\pi \\le x\\le 2\\pi .$ Express ${\\int }_{C}xdx-(x-y)dy$ as a repeated integral." 13194139535483 true] [17592186351544 1113 #uuid "43097466-6b45-5435-93fb-440587805a9f" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351544 1145 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066044/assets/264cb888010525848f647669653d34383535dd32e6ac264927105540c3b85f9f.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x$ and $Q(x,y)=-(x-y).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y):\\pi \\le x\\le 2\\pi ,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\le y\\le 0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the Green's Theorem we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}xdx-(x-y)dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}^{0}dy\\,dx.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535483 true] [17592186351545 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351546 13194139535483 true] [17592186351545 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351547 13194139535483 true] [17592186351545 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351548 13194139535483 true] [17592186351545 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351549 13194139535483 true] [17592186351545 1000 #edb/ref 17592186351550 13194139535483 true] [17592186351545 1040 #edb/ref 1759218604545